        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 12,
        "titulo": "Je le lui donne",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: full order before the verb: me/te/se/nous/vous → le/la/les → lui/leur → y → en. STEP 2: two pronouns: «Je te le donne.» «Il me les envoie.» «Je le lui donne.» STEP 3: «y» and «en» come last: «Il y en a trois.» STEP 4: the negation surrounds the block: «Je ne te le donne pas.» STEP 5: passé composé: the block comes before the auxiliary: «Je le lui ai donné.» STEP 6: with an infinitive, the block goes before the infinitive: «Je vais te le dire.» STEP 7: affirmative imperative: verb + le/la/les + moi/toi/lui/nous/vous/leur + y + en: «Donne-le-moi !» «Donnez-les-leur !» «Vas-y !» «Donne-m'en !» STEP 8: the negative imperative goes back to the normal order: «Ne me le donne pas !»",
        "comparacao": "in English, object pronouns follow the verb (“give it to him”). In French they come before it, in a fixed «waiting line»: me/te → le/la/les → lui/leur → y → en. Practise aloud until it becomes automatic; it is the part of the grammar that sounds most like French.",
